    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d638">

                <pb facs="0937" n="927" xml:id="pg927"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/1–2646: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-01-26T12:00:00+07:00">January 26,
                            1946—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received January 27—3:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>165. From General Marshall. Modification of 2(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) of
                    my 2241 of December 29<anchor n="48" xml:id="d638-f1"/> in NAC resolution of
                    January 15, substantially acceptable to me. (Reurtel 117 of January 18.) Should
                    the Chinese response to the draft treaty show any wide divergences between their
                    approach and ours the extension of credit might then be made dependent on
                    conclusion of a satisfactory treaty. We will be better able to determine the
                    appropriate kind of action after the Chinese have presented their response to
                    the draft treaty and negotiations commence with the arrival of Wilson.<anchor
                        n="49" xml:id="d638-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>2. I concur with the consensus of view in Washington that it is inappropriate to
                    make a set aside for later Eximbank credit for China at present. [Marshall.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d638-f1" n="48" xml:id="d638-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1945v07#pg_1198">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vii</hi>, p. 1198</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d638-f2" n="49" xml:id="d638-f2-content">
                <p>Robert R. Wilson, of the Division of Commercial Policy, sent to China to serve as
                    one of the negotiators of the commercial treaty.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
